// Expected: typeinfer should reject comparing a bool against an int.
//
// The runtime compares the VMValue's type tag first, so `b == 1` is
// unconditionally false and `b != 0` is unconditionally TRUE. Neither is
// visible at the call site, which is how `lib/test.tpr`'s `assert` shipped
// for months as a silent no-op: the parameter was declared `int cond`, every
// natural caller passed a bool (`assert(x < y, ...)`), and `cond == 0` could
// never hold. A whole test suite was green while asserting nothing.
//
// Strict mode should turn each of these into exit 1 with a `[typecheck]` line
// saying the comparison is always false / always true.

bool b = true;

if (b == 1) {
    print("unreachable");
}
if (b != 0) {
    print("always taken");
}
